            <ArticleTitle>Pain relief caused by millimeter waves in mice: results of cold water tail flick tests.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>575-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To find out if millimeter waves can decrease experimental pain response in mice using cold water tail flick test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Male Swiss albino mice (15 mice per group) were exposed to continuous millimeter waves at a frequency of 61.22 GHz with incident power densities (IPD) ranging from 0.15 to 5.0 mW/cm2 for 15 min or sham exposed. Latency of tail withdrawal in a cold water (1 +/- 0.5 degrees C) tail flick test was measured before the exposure (baseline) and then four times after the exposure with 15 min breaks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean latency of the tail flick response in mice exposed to millimeter waves was more than twice that of sham-exposed controls (p&lt;0.05). This effect was proportional to the power of millimeter waves and completely disappeared at an IPD level of &lt; or = 0.5 mW/cm2. Pretreatment of mice with the opioid antagonist naloxone (1 mg/kg i.p.) blocked the effect of millimeter waves.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Results suggest that the antinociceptive effect of millimeter waves is mediated through endogenous opioids.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
